<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="benzoyl-β-D-glucopyranose biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7468" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Avenacins are triterpene glucosides which act as phytoalexins and allelopathic agents conferring resistance against soil-borne pathogens and influencing the development of close neighbors |CITS:[10536024] [16995908]|. The occurrence of the phytoalexic avenacins appears to be restricted to the genus &lt;i&gt;Avena&lt;/i&gt; which is an expression of a highly specialized defense biochemistry |CITS:[Hostettmann95]|. In general, triterpenes are valued compounds with a wide range of commercial applications and because of that in the focus of metabolic engineering. One of the major goals is to re-introduce lost traits of resistance into cultivated crops |CITS:[22639586]|. As a general rule genes in plant biochemical pathways, unlike bacterial biochemical pathways, have been regarded as unlinked. However, the avenacin biosynthesis appears to be carried out by an avenacin biosynthetic gene cluster which implies a high degree of coordination regarding avenacin synthesis, transport and sequestration, all of which must be precisely orchestrated in time and space between different cell organelles and compartments |CITS:[21876149] [15148404] [21443624] [20040536]|. The formation of |FRAME:CPD-12630| (this pathway) as acyl-donor is critical for the biosynthesis of |FRAME:CPD-16599| (see |FRAME:PWY-7474|) and the other |FRAME:BENZOATE| acylated avenacin, i.e. |FRAME:CPD-16598| |CITS: [Crombie86a]|. It has been demonstrated that the C-21 acylation of the avenacin skeleton is pivotal for the biological activity of this antimicrobial agent |CITS:[20835386]|. The glucosylation of |FRAME:BENZOATE| is catalyzed by two glucosyltransferases, SAD10 (UGT74H5) and UGT74H6 which are capable to glucosylate both |FRAME:ANTHRANILATE| and |FRAME:BENZOATE|. SAD10 appears to be included in the avenacin biosynthetic gene cluster but UGT74H6, although catalyzing the same reaction, albeit with lower efficiency, may have other or additional biochemical functions in &lt;i&gt;Avena&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[23258535]|. Co-expression of three SAD gens, i.e. SAD9 (anthranilate N-methyltransferase), SAD10 (N-methylanthranilate glucosyltransferase) and SAD7 (des-acyl avenacin A acyltransferase) demonstrated that those SAD genes are involved in consecutive steps of the acylation of avenacin and that a loss of any of them resulted in enhanced disease susceptibility of the respective mutant |CITS:[23532069]|.</Description>
